From 18b520fd53ccb023afcaa973ddc40a867f75b18e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Techniix <Techniix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:29:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] app_server: show drag bitmaps with a hardware cursor

With a hardware cursor, a drag bitmap (an icon dragged in Tracker, for
example) is never shown: the accelerant is only given the cursor itself,
and AccelerantHWInterface::_DrawCursor() draws nothing while
fHardwareCursorEnabled, so the drag bitmap that _AdoptDragBitmap()
combines with the cursor is never drawn. This affects every accelerant
that exports B_SET_CURSOR_BITMAP, or B_SET_CURSOR_SHAPE for BCursor
cursors.

Use the software cursor while a drag bitmap is shown and the hardware
cursor again when the drag ends:

 * SetCursor() moves its hook handling into _UpdateHardwareCursor(),
   which doesn't use the hardware while a drag bitmap the software
   cursor can show is set (_DragBitmapShown(): the same tests as
   _AdoptDragBitmap(), including its color spaces). When it switches
   from hardware to software the cursor is now drawn
   (_DrawSoftwareCursor()); HWInterface only draws it when the cursor or
   drag bitmap changes.
 * New SetDragBitmap() override: under the exclusive lock, calls
   HWInterface::SetDragBitmap() (which draws into the frame buffer and
   used to do so without that lock), then switches between hardware and
   software if a switch is due. EventDispatcher clears the drag bitmap
   after every drag; normally nothing needs switching then (it only
   tries the hardware again if the cursor is in software).
 * SetCursor() keeps the exclusive lock until HWInterface::SetCursor()
   has set fCursor, so SetDragBitmap() can't hand the hardware an older
   cursor in between. The lock is recursive.

Also, SetCursor() called show_cursor(cursorSet) after every cursor
change, which made a hidden cursor (SetCursorVisible(false), or obscured
while typing) reappear on the next shape change. It now passes
cursorSet && the cursor's visibility; SetCursorVisible() shows it again,
including when an obscured cursor is moved.

Lock order: SetDragBitmap() is called with the EventDispatcher lock held
and takes the engine lock and then fFloatingOverlaysLock. The
EventDispatcher -> engine order already exists (MouseFilter ->
Desktop::SetCursor(), and MoveCursorTo() from the event loop when there
is no cursor thread); no path takes them the other way round.

This change was written by an AI assistant (Claude, Anthropic) at the
request of a Haiku user and has only been syntax-checked and reviewed,
not built or run. Haiku's policy does not accept AI-generated
contributions; it is offered as a description of the problem and one
possible fix, for a developer to rewrite or discard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
